## Proof

**Step 1: Reduce to one variable.** Set $t = x/y > 0$ (since $x,y>0$). Dividing both sides of
$$(c-1)x^{a+1}\le (cy-x)y^{a}$$
by $y^{a+1}>0$ gives the equivalent condition
$$(c-1)t^{a+1}\le c-t,\qquad\text{for every }t>0.$$
Define
$$g(t):=(c-1)t^{a+1}+t-c.$$
We need $g(t)\le 0$ for all $t>0$.

**Step 2: Use the equality at $t=1$.** Observe
$$g(1)=(c-1)+1-c=0.$$
So $t=1$ always gives equality. Since we require $g(t)\le 0$ for every $t>0$ and $g(1)=0$, the point $t=1$ is a **global maximum** of $g$ on $(0,\infty)$. Because $g$ is differentiable, Fermat's theorem gives $g'(1)=0$. Computing,
$$g'(t)=(c-1)(a+1)t^{a}+1,\qquad g'(1)=(c-1)(a+1)+1.$$
Setting $g'(1)=0$:
$$(c-1)(a+1)+1=0\;\Longrightarrow\; c-1=-\frac{1}{a+1}\;\Longrightarrow\; c=\frac{a}{a+1}.$$
Hence $C_a\subseteq\left\{\dfrac{a}{a+1}\right\}$.

**Step 3: Verify $c=\dfrac{a}{a+1}$ works.** With $c=\dfrac{a}{a+1}$ we have $c-1=-\dfrac{1}{a+1}$, so
$$g(t)=\frac{-t^{a+1}+(a+1)t-a}{a+1}=\frac{p(t)}{a+1},\qquad p(t):=-t^{a+1}+(a+1)t-a.$$
Now $p(1)=-1+(a+1)-a=0$, and
$$p'(t)=-(a+1)t^{a}+(a+1)=(a+1)(1-t^{a}).$$
For $0<t<1$, since $a>0$, we have $t^{a}<1$, so $p'(t)>0$ — $p$ is increasing. For $t>1$, $t^{a}>1$, so $p'(t)<0$ — $p$ is decreasing. Therefore $p(t)\le p(1)=0$ for all $t>0$, which gives $g(t)\le 0$ for all $t>0$. The inequality is satisfied.

**Step 4: Conclusion.** Combining Steps 2 and 3,
$$C_a=\left\{\frac{a}{a+1}\right\}.$$
The unique value of $c$ for which the inequality holds for all positive $x,y$ is $c=\dfrac{a}{a+1}$. Expressing $C_a$ in the form $[k,\infty)$ (a degenerate ray whose left endpoint is the only admissible value), we obtain
$$\boxed{k=\dfrac{a}{a+1}}.$$

